 sections=[
  S("Residual hybrids, step by step", "<p>(1) Fit or choose the physics model, here Greenshields v = v<sub>f</sub>(1 − ρ/ρ<sub>j</sub>). (2) Compute residuals r = v<sub>data</sub> − v<sub>physics</sub>. (3) Fit a small, regularised model to the residuals (here a quadratic). (4) Predict v = v<sub>physics</sub> + r̂. Keep the correction small and smooth; a huge learned correction means the physics is wrong, not just imperfect.</p>",
    ("Residual hybrid", "learn", "Learn the gap.", [("Physics first", "Greenshields."), ("Fit residuals", "Small, smooth."), ("Add them", "Prediction.")])),
  S("Beyond the data", "<p>With 20 observations up to 60 veh/km, the errors at higher densities are: ML only (degree-4 polynomial) about 870 km/h, physics only about 13 km/h, hybrid about 3.6 km/h. With data up to 100 veh/km all improve, but the hybrid stays best. The explorer lets you see how quickly the ML-only model goes wrong once it leaves its data.</p>",
    ("Results", "data", "Hybrid wins beyond data.", [("ML only: 870 km/h", "Wild."), ("Physics only: 13", "Biased."), ("Hybrid: 3.6", "Best of both.")])),
  S("Other hybrid patterns", "<p><b>Parameter learning</b>: a network predicts physical parameters from context (for example, capacity from weather and lighting), then the physics model runs. <b>Closure learning</b>: a network represents an unresolved term inside the equation, trained through the solver (universal differential equations). <b>Gray-box models</b> mix known and learned blocks in a system diagram. All keep interpretable structure.</p>",
    ("Patterns", "learn", "Three families.", [("Parameter learning", "Context → parameters."), ("Closure learning", "Inside the equation."), ("Gray box", "Mixed blocks.")])),
  S("When to choose a hybrid", "<p>Choose a hybrid when the physics captures the main structure but misses details, data are limited, and extrapolation matters (new conditions, rare events). Avoid it if the physics is badly wrong (the correction will dominate) or if data are abundant and conditions never change (pure ML may be simpler).</p>",
    ("Choosing", "apply", "Good physics, limited data.", [("Main structure right", "Physics."), ("Limited data", "ML helper."), ("Extrapolation needed", "Hybrid wins.")])),
 ],
